Excitation energy of hydrogen like ion in its first excitation state, $\Delta E=40.8 \mathrm{eV}$
For the hydrogen like ion, energy in the ground state $(n=1)$,

E=\frac{-13.6 Z^{2}}{n^{2}} \mathrm{eV}=-13.6 Z^{2} \mathrm{eV}

Energy in the first excited state, $E_{2}=\frac{-13.6 Z^{2}}{n^{2}}$
=\frac{-13.6 Z^{2}}{4}

Excitation energy, $\Delta E=E_{2}-E_{1}$
\begin{aligned}
&=\left(\frac{-13.6 Z^{2}}{4}+13.6 Z^{2}\right) \mathrm{eV} \\
&=(-3.4+13.6) Z^{2} \mathrm{eV} \\
\Rightarrow \quad 40.8 \mathrm{eV} &=10.2 Z^{2} \mathrm{eV} \\
\Rightarrow \quad \quad Z^{2} &=\frac{40.8}{10.2} \\
\Rightarrow \quad \quad Z^{2} &=4 \\
\Rightarrow \quad \quad Z &=2
\end{aligned}

Thus, energy of the electron in the ground state is given as

\begin{array}{ll}
\Rightarrow & E_{1}=-13.6(2)^{2} \mathrm{eV} \\
\Rightarrow & E_{1}=-54.4 \mathrm{eV}
\end{array}

Hence, the energy needed to remove the electron from the ion in ground state (ionisation energy)

=-(-54.4) \mathrm{eV}=54.4 \mathrm{eV}

